@government
title: Government and Constitution

= Government and Constitution

The cabinet, {!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!},
{!<span class="term term-senate">!}Senate{!</span>!}, and
{!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Crown{!</span>!} exercised distinct,
overlapping powers. Parliamentary factions indicated a candidate for prime
minister; the {!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Shah{!</span>!} issued the
decree to form a ministry; and the cabinet returned to the
{!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!} for confidence.

The 1949 constitutional revision strengthened royal authority, including a
power to dissolve the {!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!}.
The First {!<span class="term term-senate">!}Senate{!</span>!} had thirty
royally appointed and thirty elected members. Neither chamber can be reduced
to a single party bloc.

Further reading:
{!<a href="timeline.html#the-constitutional-framework">!}The constitutional
framework{!</a>!};
Mostafa Elm, *Oil, Power, and Principle*, chapter 3, pp. 79–86; and
Fakhreddin Azimi, *Iran: The Crisis of Democracy*, chapter 5, pp. 260–267.

